Experimentally, we can obtain the equilibrium concentration of the chemical species involved in an equilibrium using, for example, absorption spectroscopy. After the reactants are mixed in a given quantity, in the absence of products, the forward reaction and reverse reaction proceed until they reach a state of equilibrium, where both rates are equal. At this point, the quantity of reactants would be less than the initial mixture. This is because a small amount of these reactants will decompose and form products. Therefore, the quantity of products would increase with an increase corresponding to the amount of reactants that have decomposed. Using spectroscopy, one can measure the amount of product formed, for example,  and hence elucidate the amount of reactants decomposed. One can measure the amount of reactants in equilibrium. By knowing the initial amount we mix and the resulting equilibrium concentration, we can elucidate the reaction extent. On one hand, stoichiometry plays a role in the final results of these quantities as the amounts of reactants decomposed need to form products respecting the reaction stoichiometry. On the other hand, the numerical value of the equilibrium constant $K_c$, characteristic of a reaction at a given temperature, is also critical. As the equilibrium concentrations of reacants and products should respect $K_c$. The ICE method is a tabular method used to obtain the extent of a reaction. This method leads to a mathematical function, a polynomial. Its roots give the numerical value of the extent of a reaction, and at the same time allow the calculation of the equilibrium concentration of all species involved in the chemical equilibrium.

 